*.jks
keystore.properties
local.properties
build/
.gradle/
.kotlin/
*.apk
# ...except the one the phones download. Build outputs stay ignored; this is the
# published artefact the update manifest points at.
!release/Ours.apk
# ...and the ones the F-Droid repo serves. These are the repository's payload, not build
# output: the signed index names each by hash, so an APK missing here is an entry the
# client will try to download and fail on.
!fdroid/repo/*.apk
!fdroid/archive/*.apk
app/src/main/res/raw/sheet_sync_script.txt

# The F-Droid repo's own signing key, and the config file holding its passwords. Same
# deal as ours-release.jks: gitignored, absent from a clone, and losing it is not
# recoverable by rebuilding — every client that added the repo would have to remove and
# re-add it, because the fingerprint they pinned is this key's. See docs/HANDOVER.md.
fdroid/keystore.p12
fdroid/config.yml
fdroid/tmp/
fdroid/unsigned/
fdroid/srclibs/
fdroid/.fdroid.*

# `fdroid update` leaves a run report next to the index, and it is a report about the
# machine, not the repo: home directory, Linux distribution and version, absolute paths
# to apksigner, jarsigner and keytool, and the full command line. Harmless on a private
# build server; this one is published to the open web on every push.
fdroid/repo/status/
fdroid/archive/status/
